cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Actaira in academic work, or reproduce its measurements, please
  cite it as below. The figures a paper is most likely to quote are in
  docs/FIGURES.md, which is generated by scripts/figures.py from the source
  tree itself: every figure is regenerated by `make figures` from the tree it
  describes, and `make release-check` refuses a tree whose figures and code
  disagree. Cite the version together with the commit, because a version is a
  claim about an interface and a commit is the thing that was measured.
title: "Actaira: local-first AI assurance for model artifacts and agents"
abstract: >-
  Actaira reads an AI system without running it, records what it observed as
  evidence with a lifetime, decides under a versioned policy, and signs a
  receipt a third party can verify offline. Nothing is ever loaded,
  deserialised or executed, and nothing is ever scored.
  Static inspection covers pickle protocols 0 to 5, PyTorch zip checkpoints,
  safetensors, ONNX, GGUF, NumPy .npy and Keras/HDF5, with format detection by
  content; the default import policy is an allowlist of the callables tensor
  deserialisation needs, because a denylist fails open, and the evaluation
  harness prices both. What each control looked at is published per coverage
  surface rather than collapsed into a verdict, and a surface nobody undertook
  to read cannot make a verdict inconclusive.
  On top of inspection: an evidence lifecycle in which supersession is bound to
  a subject's digest rather than to its name; a relation graph whose every edge
  carries the document that stated it, with impact answered as a route;
  governance over agent declarations, including capability rules and an
  attack-path search that reports what would break each route; policy as code,
  where a predicate with no information goes to REVIEW rather than quietly
  returning false; trust kept separate from cryptography, so that a signature
  verifying is a fact about bytes and whether this environment accepts the
  signer is a local decision; attestation over an RFC 6962 Merkle tree with
  Ed25519 signatures, an optional RFC 3161 time anchor and an in-toto Statement
  in a DSSE envelope; and executable EU AI Act controls over an obligation
  catalogue on four checkability tiers, each obligation carrying a written
  reason for the tier it is on.
  The verifier reports what it did not check - an unanchored signing key as
  embedded_key_only, an unvalidated authority chain as tsa_chain not_verified -
  rather than showing a tick for it, and the tool refuses to emit a compliance
  score: a test greps every finished document for score, grade, rating and
  percent and fails on any of them.
repository-code: "https://github.com/marcosmatalab/actaira"
